      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While a statistical correlation shows predictive power, asserting that using specific demographic concentrations leads to a \"more accurate\" model is philosophically and legally fraught. The model might be statistically efficient, but its *use* in discrimination-based pricing is often prohibited because these variables serve as proxies for protected characteristics. Instead of the specific ethnicity, try engineering interaction terms between location features (like density) and external risk factors (like proximity to transit) to capture localized behavioral differences abstractly. This retains predictive power while minimizing the risk of biased attribution.",
